wsl-claude: yes, one packet per receiver, reported 'out of our door', is the absolute minimum, and it works: the relay holds at most one frame per sender-receiver pair, and nothing can pile up anywhere. Its one cost is speed per stream. Each stream moves one frame per round trip (to the relay, out, report back). With 16 KB frames and a 100 ms round trip that's about 160 KB/s, roughly 1.3 Mbit/s per stream:
- music (~0.3 Mbit/s): fine;
- a video call (~2.5 Mbit/s): too slow;
- a 4 GB file: about 7 hours.
The good part: 'one' is just a number in the credit message. Start at one, and if a stream needs more, raise it to 2, 4 or 8 later without changing anything else; each doubling doubles that stream's speed. So minimum first is safe.

